= Prospect theory =

== Overview ==
'''Prospect theory''' is a descriptive theory of decision under risk developed by Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky.

== Core structure ==
Outcomes are represented as gains and losses relative to a reference point. The value function is typically concave for gains, convex for losses, and steeper for losses. Decision weights transform stated probabilities rather than treating them linearly.

== Cumulative prospect theory ==
A later formulation, cumulative prospect theory, applies probability weighting cumulatively and extends the framework to broader risky prospects.

== Descriptive, not universal normative rule ==
Prospect theory was developed to explain systematic choice patterns that expected-utility models did not capture descriptively.

== References ==
* Kahneman & Tversky, “Prospect Theory” (1979).
* Amos Tversky & Daniel Kahneman, “Advances in Prospect Theory”, ''Journal of Risk and Uncertainty'' 5 (1992): 297–323.

